# November 22, 2026: Tacoma, Washington (region wa). The names came from one walk-through video about the city's old
# buildings (Old World Exploration, 2025), taken as tips only: every fact below is from a source opened for this batch.
# Sixteen standing buildings and one park, nearly all downtown and in the Stadium District: Old City Hall, the Northern
# Pacific office building, the Bostwick, the Pantages and Rialto theatres, the Elks Temple, the Perkins and Sprague
# buildings, the 1908 armory, the Carnegie library, Urban Grace (First Baptist) church, Stadium and Lincoln high schools,
# the closed Holy Rosary church, the Rust mansion and Wright Park with its 1907 conservatory.
# Sources: Wikipedia as raw text and its Pierce County Register list; the City of Tacoma Register of Historic Places
# (list updated January 5, 2026); HistoryLink; Tales of Tacoma (Tacoma Public Library); Seattle Met; the Prairie Line
# Trail page of the City of Tacoma; Lee & Associates and the Old City Hall developer's page; the pages of McMenamins,
# Urban Grace and Tacoma Public Library for hours.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
BATCH={'key':'tacoma_zudq','label':'Tacoma: old downtown and Stadium District','added':'2026-11-22 21:33'}
REG=L('City of Tacoma Register of Historic Places (list of January 5, 2026)','https://cms.tacoma.gov/planning/historic-preservation/landmarks/publication%20landmarks%20register%202026-01-05%20with%20links.pdf')
DT='Downtown Tacoma: park on the street or in one of the many lots OpenStreetMap maps within a few hundred metres (owners and fees not checked).'
PLACES=[
N('old-city-hall-tacoma','Old City Hall','Tacoma','wa','civic','standing',47.25758,-122.44004,'exact','view','walk','','1893',
  'Tacoma\'s city hall from 1893 to 1959, designed by E. A. Hatherton, who had come north from San Francisco in 1891. It is five storeys on a trapezoid plot, with a ten-storey clock tower on the corner of Pacific Avenue and South 7th Street, and walls eight feet thick at the base. In 2019 the city chose Surge Tacoma to buy and redevelop it, turning it into restaurants, shops, offices and small flats.',
  'Light grey Wilkeson stone foundation, then red brick faced with yellow Roman brick, arched windows on the fourth floor and rows of small round windows. The freestanding tower has a clock on each face and a set of four bells. In April 2026 the developer\'s agents said the first phase (floors two to four: restaurants, shops and offices) was due to open that year; whether any of it is open now was not checked, so interior not checked.',
  'The clock tower that closes the view north up Commerce Street.',
  'Privately owned since 2019 and under conversion; look from Commerce Street, Pacific Avenue and South 7th Street. '+DT,
  [W('Old City Hall (Tacoma, Washington)'),REG,L('Lee & Associates: first tenants at Old City Hall (April 2026)','https://www.lee-associates.com/northwest/2026/04/09/new-kid-friendly-coworking-company-inks-lease-at-tacomas-old-city-hall/'),L('Surge Old City Hall','https://oldcityhalltacoma.com')],
  brick='no'),
N('northern-pacific-office-building-tacoma','Northern Pacific Office Building','Tacoma','wa','rail','standing',47.25756,-122.43929,'exact','view','walk','','1886–1888',
  'The Tacoma division headquarters of the Northern Pacific Railway, on the bluff above the rail yards and Commencement Bay, across from the old city hall. Built of brick, stucco, stone and cast iron in the Second Empire style, it held 53 offices and 19 fireproof vaults. Later used as a city hall annex, it lost its four-storey southern block in the 1970s (Wikipedia gives both 1974 and 1975) when Pacific Avenue was widened.',
  'What stands is the triangular northern part: a round four-storey tower at the north tip with a lantern on top, and three storeys behind it on an ashlar stone base. The cut end on the south is a plain brick and concrete wall. Interior not checked.',
  'The railroad that made Tacoma, in its own office on the bluff.',
  'An office building; look from Pacific Avenue and South 7th Street. '+DT,
  [W('Northern Pacific Office Building'),REG],
  brick='no'),
N('bostwick-building-tacoma','Bostwick Building (Hotel Bostwick)','Tacoma','wa','hotel','standing',47.25567,-122.44090,'exact','view','walk','','1889',
  'A wedge-shaped hotel and shop block built in 1889 by Henry Clay Bostwick on the point where St. Helens Avenue, Broadway and 9th Street meet, opened in 1890 with the Western Trust Company on the ground floor and three floors of hotel rooms above. It is the only one left of three buildings put up together at this corner; the other two, the Tacoma Theater and the Gross Bros. store, are gone.',
  'Gables over every other bay joined by a parapet round the whole building. The board-and-moulding panels of the walls were covered with stucco in 1924, when two bays were taken off the north end; the stucco was painted yellow with red trim in the 1980s. Shops on the ground floor, which held coffee shops from 1993 until the Bostwick Cafe closed in 2023. Interior not checked.',
  'A flatiron hotel of the 1889 boom.',
  'Private; seen from the sidewalks round it. '+DT,
  [L('Seattle Met: the history of the Bostwick','https://www.seattlemet.com/news-and-city-life/tacoma-bostwick-building-history/'),W('Old City Hall Historic District'),REG],
  brick='no'),
N('pantages-theater-tacoma','Pantages Theater (Jones Building)','Tacoma','wa','civic','standing',47.25505,-122.44016,'exact','open','walk','','1916–1918',
  'A vaudeville theatre and office building commissioned by Alexander Pantages in 1916 and opened in January 1918, designed by B. Marcus Priteca with Edwin W. Houghton. The city register lists it as Pantages Theatre and Jones Building. It was remodelled in 1955, renovated in 1982–1983 and given a larger stage in 2014, and is still a working theatre.',
  'White glazed terra cotta fronts on 9th Street and Broadway, in a Second Renaissance Revival dress over a steel and concrete frame; the Commerce Street side is yellowish brick. Above the ground floor the outside is largely unaltered. The auditorium can be seen at performances.',
  '',
  'A working theatre: the inside is seen with a ticket to a show. The operator\'s pages could not be opened when this was written, so no hours are given. '+DT,
  [W('Pantages Theatre (Tacoma)'),REG],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('rialto-theater-tacoma','Rialto Theater','Tacoma','wa','civic','standing',47.25495,-122.44158,'exact','open','walk','','1918',
  'A movie palace built in 1918 for Henry T. Moore to designs by Tacoma architect Roland E. Borhek, first for 1,500 people and now seating 742. It stands on 9th Street, which climbs a full storey across its front. It was put on the National Register in 1992 and still holds performances.',
  'A freestanding classical front sheathed in white glazed terra cotta. Inside, most of Borhek\'s design survives: lobby, auditorium with balcony and proscenium stage; the storefronts and marquee have gone and the lobby\'s decorated ceiling is hidden. A mural covers the back wall (2010).',
  '',
  'A working theatre, seen inside at performances; no hours page could be read when this was written. '+DT,
  [W('Rialto Theater (Tacoma, Washington)'),REG],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('elks-temple-tacoma','Elks Temple','Tacoma','wa','civic','standing',47.25804,-122.44094,'exact','open','walk','','1916',
  'A Beaux-Arts lodge built in 1916 for the Elks to designs by Édouard Frère Champney. It stood empty and derelict for years until McMenamins bought it in 2007; restoration began in earnest in 2017 and it reopened in April 2019 as a hotel with restaurants, bars and event rooms. The Spanish Steps beside it were built as the fire exit from the lodge\'s second storey and the city kept them open to the public in 1936.',
  'The restored building is open to the public as a hotel, pub and music venue; the old main lodge room became hotel rooms. Walk the Spanish Steps on its south side.',
  '',
  'Owned and run by McMenamins at 565 Broadway; the pub hours are from its page (read November 2026). '+DT,
  [W('Elks Temple (Tacoma, Washington)'),L('McMenamins Pub at Elks Temple','https://www.mcmenamins.com/elks-temple/mcmenamins-pub-at-elks-temple')],
  brick='no',hours='Pub: Sunday to Thursday 7 a.m. to 11 p.m., Friday and Saturday 7 a.m. to midnight'),
N('perkins-building-tacoma','Perkins Building','Tacoma','wa','civic','standing',47.25333,-122.43690,'exact','view','walk','','1907–1910',
  'An eight-storey newspaper plant built in 1907 for the Tacoma Ledger and Daily News and named for its founder Sam Perkins; the southern half was added in 1909 (the city register gives 1907–10). Wikipedia calls it the tallest reinforced concrete building in the Northwest of its day and the first on the West Coast with a basement garage. The University of Washington Tacoma began here in 1990; it was converted to flats, offices and shops in 2002–2004.',
  'A Chicago School block on a rigid concrete frame with a brick face, at 1101 A Street. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private flats and offices; seen from A Street and 11th Street. '+DT,
  [W('Perkins Building'),REG],
  brick='?'),
N('sprague-building-tacoma','Sprague Building','Tacoma','wa','civic','standing',47.24906,-122.43721,'exact','view','walk','','1889–1890',
  'One of the first warehouses in Tacoma, built for John Sprague, railroad official and first mayor of the joined city, to store goods waiting for ships or trains once the line to the Midwest was finished in 1888. Its first tenants sold builders\' and carriage hardware, logging and blacksmith supplies, ship chandlery, boilers and blasting powder. The city register gives 1889–90 and the architects Pickles and Sutton.',
  'The Pacific Avenue front has the good materials and detail; the plain back faced the railroad, now the Prairie Line Trail, where goods went up a freight elevator from a spur line. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private; seen from Pacific Avenue and from the Prairie Line Trail behind it. '+DT,
  [L('City of Tacoma, Prairie Line Trail: Sprague Building','https://prairielinetrail.org/site/sprague-building-'),REG],
  brick='yes'),
N('tacoma-armory','Tacoma Armory','Tacoma','wa','civic','standing',47.25265,-122.44674,'exact','view','walk','','1908',
  'One of the first three National Guard armories in Washington, with Spokane and Seattle, completed in 1908 according to HistoryLink (the city register gives 1909, architects Russell and Babcock). Troops trained and mustered in its drill hall for both world wars, and it held graduations, boxing and wrestling, dances and concerts. Its military use ended in 2011; the state has since sold its old armories, and this one is used for events.',
  'A large castle-like building with a big open drill hall, at 715 South 11th Street. It is seen inside only at events held there. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Seen from South 11th Street; open only for ticketed events, and no hours were found. Parking not checked.',
  [L('HistoryLink: National Guard armories in Washington','https://www.historylink.org/file/10452'),L('Tales of Tacoma: the Tacoma Armory','https://tacomatales.org/2022/12/31/the-tacoma-armory/'),REG],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('carnegie-library-tacoma','Tacoma Main Library (Carnegie building)','Tacoma','wa','civic','standing',47.25240,-122.44479,'exact','open','walk','','1903',
  'Opened on June 4, 1903, the first Carnegie library built in Washington, designed by Jardine, Kent and Jardine. Its dome was damaged in the 1949 earthquake and later mostly removed. A large wing was added in 1952; the old part was renovated in 1988–1990 and the wing in 2023–2025. It is still the main library of Tacoma and holds the Northwest Room archives.',
  'The 1903 building with the 1952 wing behind it, at 1102 Tacoma Avenue South. It is a working public library, so the public goes in during opening hours.',
  'The state\'s first Carnegie library, still lending books.',
  'Tacoma Public Library; the hours are from its Main Library page (read November 2026), which gave only some days. Street parking not checked.',
  [W('Tacoma Public Library'),L('Tacoma Public Library: Main Library','https://www.tacomalibrary.org/locations/1'),REG],
  brick='?',hours='Tuesday noon to 8 p.m., Thursday 10 a.m. to 6 p.m.; other days on the library\'s page'),
N('urban-grace-church-tacoma','Urban Grace Church (First Baptist Church)','Tacoma','wa','worship','standing',47.25483,-122.44235,'exact','open','walk','','1924–1925',
  'Built as the First Baptist Church in a Gothic Revival style to designs by Heath, Gove and Bell; Wikipedia gives 1924, the city register 1925. Its 53 rooms include a 1,250-seat auditorium, a banquet hall, a chapel and choir rooms. The congregation, now interdenominational and called Urban Grace, still meets here.',
  'The Gothic church at 902 Market Street. The public can come in at the Sunday service. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Urban Grace, 902 Market Street; the service time is from the church\'s page (read November 2026). '+DT,
  [W('Urban Grace Church'),L('Urban Grace: who we are','https://www.urbangracetacoma.org/about/'),REG],
  brick='no',hours='Sunday worship 10:30 a.m.'),
N('holy-rosary-church-tacoma','Holy Rosary Church','Tacoma','wa','worship','standing',47.23331,-122.43954,'exact','view','drive','','1920–1929',
  'The parish was founded in 1891 for German-speaking Catholics and served by Benedictine monks until 1998. The present church, designed by C. Frank Mahon and seen from Interstate 5, replaced the wooden one; Wikipedia gives 1920 and the city register 1929. Water damage in 2018 closed it, the parish ended in 2020 and was merged away in 2021, and in 2025 the archdiocese decreed that it may no longer be used for worship; an appeal against that was filed with the Vatican and, by Wikipedia\'s account, is still under review. It has been on the state\'s most endangered list since 2020.',
  'The tall-spired church on South 30th Street, closed. Interior not checked.',
  'A city landmark church whose fate is still undecided.',
  'Closed; look from South 30th Street. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Holy Rosary Church (Tacoma, Washington)'),REG],
  conf='mixed',brick='yes'),
N('stadium-high-school-tacoma','Stadium High School','Tacoma','wa','civic','standing',47.26625,-122.44806,'exact','view','walk','','1891; school 1906',
  'Begun in 1891 by Hewitt and Hewitt as a luxury château hotel for the Northern Pacific and the Tacoma Land Company; the Panic of 1893 stopped the work, and a fire gutted the empty shell in 1898. The school district bought it in 1904, Frederick Heath made it a school, and it opened as Tacoma High School in 1906, renamed for the stadium built in the gulch beside it in 1910.',
  'The Châteauesque building on the bluff at 111 North E Street, with the Stadium Bowl below it in the old gulch (once seating 32,000, about half of it lost in a 1970s restoration). A working high school: inside is not open to visitors.',
  'A railway hotel that became a high school.',
  'Tacoma Public Schools; look from North E Street and the streets round the bowl. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Stadium High School'),REG],
  brick='yes'),
N('lincoln-high-school-tacoma','Lincoln High School','Tacoma','wa','civic','standing',47.22500,-122.44114,'exact','view','drive','','1913',
  'Tacoma\'s second high school, first called Lincoln Park High School, on land next to Lincoln Park. Frederick Heath designed it; the cornerstone was laid on September 1, 1913 and classes began in September 1914.',
  'A Collegiate Gothic building in three wings shaped like a Y, with words carved in Wilkeson sandstone over the doors and a clock tower carrying the year 1913 in stone. A working school: inside is not open to visitors.',
  '',
  'Tacoma Public Schools, 701 South 37th Street; look from the street. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Lincoln High School (Tacoma, Washington)'),REG],
  brick='yes'),
N('rust-house-tacoma','William Ross Rust House','Tacoma','wa','landmark','standing',47.26634,-122.46318,'exact','view','drive','','1905',
  'Built in 1905 for William Ross Rust, president of the Tacoma Smelter and Refining Company, to designs by Ambrose J. Russell, and modelled on the McCall mansion in New Jersey. It has 18 rooms, four baths and eight fireplaces, and was put on the National Register in 1985.',
  'A mansion of Wilkeson sandstone under a green glazed terra cotta tile roof, at 1001 North I Street. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'A private house: look from North I Street only. Parking not checked.',
  [W('William Ross Rust House'),REG],
  brick='no'),
N('wright-park-tacoma','Wright Park and Seymour Conservatory','Tacoma','wa','inst','standing',47.26030,-122.44830,'site','public','walk','','1886; conservatory 1907',
  'A 27-acre park and arboretum laid out by E. O. Schwagerl on land given by Charles Barstow Wright, managed by Metro Parks Tacoma; the Register list dates it to 1886 and the city register to 1890. The W. W. Seymour Botanical Conservatory, designed by Isaac J. Knapp and named for its donor, was added in 1907.',
  'Over 700 mature trees of about 100 kinds, a bust of Ibsen, a pair of lions and other statues. The Victorian glass conservatory has a twelve-sided central dome and some 3,500 panes, with more than 550 kinds of plants inside.',
  '',
  'A Metro Parks Tacoma city park between Division Avenue, 6th Avenue and South G and I streets. The conservatory\'s page could not be opened when this was written, so its hours were not checked. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Wright Park (Tacoma, Washington)'),REG],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
]
